{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## THE FIX IS LANDED BUT DOES NOT WORK LIVE — and the cause is an error in MY PLAN, not in the implementation\n\nHarness run **30784592232** against main @ `de2f1ed87` (which CONTAINS this fix, merged as change 1276):\n`[jira-live-canary] collected=56 executed=56 skipped=0`, **4 failed / 52 passed** in 23m36s.\n`test_inbound_delete_link_round_trips` is STILL RED:\n\n    AssertionError: the Jira link was DELETED (confirmed absent from the search-backed link read) but\n    the local dep on jira-rbjflfv-1 still targets 'jira-rbjflfv-2': deps target ['jira-rbjflfv-2'].\n    assert 'jira-rbjflfv-2' not in {'jira-rbjflfv-2'}\n\n(The cell's message text still cites \"the inbound link differ is ADD-only\" — that string is static prose\nwritten before the fix and is NOT evidence the differ is still add-only. What the assertion PROVES is only\nthat the removal did not reach the store.)\n\n### ROOT CAUSE: G4 suppresses EVERY legitimate peer-deletion mirror. The rule I specified is wrong.\n\nThe landed G4 (`inbound_differ.py:754-781`) drops an inbound `remove` whenever the outbound side has that\ntarget in `link_add_keys`, and its comment states my reasoning verbatim: *\"an outbound ADD to this target\nmeans 'being pushed', NOT 'peer deleted it' — never mirror a removal for it.\"*\n\n**That inference does not hold, and its failure is total rather than partial.** When the peer deletes a\nlink, the local dep still exists and the peer no longer has it — which is EXACTLY the condition under\nwhich the outbound differ emits a link ADD. So on any bidirectional pass, a peer deletion always produces\nan outbound ADD for that target, and G4 therefore always suppresses the inbound REMOVE. **The feature can\nnever fire.** My plan asserted G4 was needed to close G3's blind spot and called it \"not cosmetic\"; it is\nworse than cosmetic — it is load-bearing in the wrong direction.\n\nThe implementation is faithful to the plan and the mutation ledger is sound: `test_g4_outbound_add_this_pass_suppresses_the_removal`\npasses because it asserts precisely the behaviour I specified. **A green mutation ledger cannot catch a\nwrong specification** — only the live oracle did. That is this epic's own lesson (an oracle can only refute\nwhat it was pointed at) arriving at my expense.\n\n### THE CORRECTED RULE — G3 decides PRECEDENCE; it is not a second AND-gate beside G4\n\nThe two directions disagree about the same fact, so one must win, and `managed_refs` is the signal that\nsays which:\n * ref **IS managed** (we pushed it at some point) and the peer no longer has it → **the peer deleted it**\n   → the inbound REMOVE wins, and **the outbound ADD for that pair must be suppressed.**\n * ref is **NOT managed** (never pushed) and the peer does not have it → **we simply have not pushed it**\n   → the outbound ADD wins, and the inbound REMOVE is suppressed. (This is the case G4 was written for,\n   and it is the only case it should cover.)\n\nResidual risk after the correction: `managed_refs` is monotonic and `seed_managed_refs_from_current` marks\na pre-feature ticket's CURRENT deps as managed, so a never-pushed dep on such a ticket could be read as\n\"peer deleted it\" and removed locally. That window is real and must be recorded — but it is far narrower\nthan the present behaviour, which is that the feature never works at all.\n\n### SCOPE CONSEQUENCE: the outbound side must change too\n\nSuppressing only the inbound side is not enough. Both differs run against the SAME pre-pass snapshot and\nthen both apply, so if inbound unlinks locally while outbound still adds the link in Jira, the pass ends\ndivergent and the next pass re-adopts the link inbound — churn, not convergence. So the managed case must\nalso stop the outbound ADD. That means `outbound_links.py` (or the shared suppression context) joins the\nfile impact — this is arbitration across BOTH differs, which the original plan did not contemplate.\n\n### STATUS\n\nChange 1276 is merged and is NOT being reverted: layers 1-3 (the removal differ, the apply-layer action\nbranch and `rebar.unlink` wiring, G1/G2/G3/G5) are correct and independently mutation-proven, and the\napply-layer drop it fixed was a genuine defect. Only the G3/G4 arbitration is wrong. **This ticket stays\nOPEN**; the plan is being amended to the corrected rule, re-reviewed, and a follow-up change will land the\narbitration fix. e369 cannot close until the live cell is green, per this ticket's own acceptance criterion."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785739331164516001,"uuid":"1dd020ee-0784-4724-939f-3177702e99b1"}